================================================================================
AROL AGENTIC TELEMETRY ANALYSIS
System and Device Programming - Project Q3
Multi-Agent System (MAS) for Industrial IoT Data Refinement and Analytics
================================================================================

WHAT THIS IS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A chatbot that analyses capping-machine telemetry, built so that the language
model never touches the arithmetic.

A user asks a question in plain language ("what is the success rate per head?",
"is anything wrong with head 26?"). An orchestrator agent decides which
analysis tools to call and in what order; deterministic Python functions do
every calculation; a report assembler writes the answer around the numbers.

The model chooses tools. It never sees a raw telemetry row and never computes
a value. That is what makes the output repeatable: the same question returns
the same numbers every time, and a test asserts it.


REQUIREMENTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Python 3.11 or newer, plus:

    polars, numpy, scipy, pyyaml, pyarrow, matplotlib, pytest

Install with:

    pip install -r requirements.txt

No API key and no network access are needed for ANY of this, including the
LLM planner: the model runs locally through Ollama. Nothing leaves the
machine. The default planner is the rule-based one and needs nothing at all.


HOW TO RUN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
All commands are run from the repository root.

  List the analysis tools the agent can call:
      python -m src.interface.cli tools

  List the available data pools:
      python -m src.interface.cli pools

  Generate a canned report (kpi | anomalies | idle | throughput):
      python -m src.interface.cli report kpi
      python -m src.interface.cli report anomalies

  Ask a question in plain language:
      python -m src.interface.cli ask "is anything wrong with the machine?"
      python -m src.interface.cli ask "idle periods on head 5"
      python -m src.interface.cli ask "throughput by hour"

  Interactive session:
      python -m src.interface.cli chat

  Run the scaling benchmark (objective 5):
      python -m src.testing.benchmark --sizes 1 2 4

  Rebuild the real-data cache by running Person A's own pipeline
  (needs his repo checked out beside this one, as ../person-a):
      python scripts/refresh_person_a_cache.py

  Global options (these go BEFORE the subcommand):
      --config PATH   use a different config file
      --pool NAME     analyse a specific pool
      --no-save       print only; do not write report and trace files

Reports are written to reports/ and the matching tool-call trace to logs/.


RUNNING THE TESTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      python -m pytest tests -q

The suite covers the integration contract (schema, status decoding, envelope,
registry), the KPI tools, the agent loop including its failure paths, and a
ground-truth evaluation that measures anomaly detection against faults
deliberately injected into the synthetic data.


DATA
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Three sources, selected by one key in config.yaml:

    data:
      source: synthetic     # or: person_a, real

  synthetic  Generated events with faults injected at known locations. Used
             for development and as the ground truth for evaluation. Needs no
             telemetry files. This is the default.

  person_a   Person A's real event table, read from the Parquet his pipeline
             writes and converted by src/ingestion/adapter.py. His frame
             carries 8 of the 12 contract columns and types status as Int64;
             the adapter supplies the rest and DECLARES what it cannot
             recover. Point data.person_a.<pool> at the Parquet file.

  real       Person A's in-process WP1 loader (src/ingestion/api.py). Still a
             stub; person_a is the working path today.

Everything else is identical between them: all three produce the same event
table, and the same tests run against any of them.

INPUT FORMAT (real source)
  One CSV per machine per day, ~57 MB, 86,400 rows (one sample per second).
  Columns: a single "timestamp", then three per head - "H## Count",
  "H## AppTorque", "H## Status" - for 36 heads (109 columns in total).
  Head count is auto-detected, not hard-coded.

  A closure is a +1 increment of a head's counter. Its torque and status sit
  on the same row as the increment. Files in a pool are read in timestamp
  order and treated as ONE continuous counter stream, so a closure that
  straddles midnight is still detected.

EVENT TABLE (what every analysis tool consumes)
  One row per closure event:

    ts             plant-local naive timestamp of the increment
    pool_id        which pool the event came from
    machine_id     parsed from the filename
    head_id        H01 .. H36
    head_index     1 .. 36, for numeric sort order
    torque         AppTorque on the increment row, Nm
    status         raw status code
    error_class    decoded name (Closure OK / No Load / Bad Closure / Unknown)
    reject_signal  status bit 0
    cap_present    TRI-STATE. True = a cap was in the head, False = nothing
                   was, NULL = the status code does not say. Never guessed:
                   a guess of True would silently enter the success-rate
                   denominator and change every rate
    count_delta    counter increment that produced this event
    inferred       True when count_delta > 1 (a dropped poll)

STATUS CODES
  Decoded as a BITFIELD, not an enum: bit 0 is the reject flag and the high
  bits are the error category, so 65 == 64 | 1. Every category appears as a
  pair n / n+1 with the same meaning, rejected or not.

    0    Closure OK        a cap was applied successfully, torque about 2.0 Nm
    2    No Load           the head cycled with no cap present, torque about 0
    4    No Closure
    8    No InTorque
    16   No CapTurns
    32   Following Error
    64   Bad Closure       (65 = 64|1 is the rejected form, and what occurs)

  Seen in the real February data so far: 0, 2, 65 and - in four day-files,
  twice - 9. Code 9 == 8|1 is No InTorque, rejected. It was decoded correctly
  with no code change, which is the argument for the bitfield: an enum built
  from the observed codes would have returned "Unknown (9)". Anything genuinely
  unknown still decodes to "Unknown (<code>)" and is marked unconfirmed rather
  than crashing.

  IMPORTANT: the success rate uses cap-present closures as its denominator.
  A No Load cycle is not a failed closure - there was no cap to close - so
  counting it as a failure understates performance badly. On the real
  2026-02-01 data the two denominators differ by 44.1 percentage points
  (99.9991% against 55.87%). Reports always state which was used and give
  both numbers.


CONFIGURATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Everything lives in config.yaml; nothing is hard-coded. Copy it to
config.local.yaml to override values locally without touching the shared file
(config.local.yaml is gitignored).

    data.source        synthetic | person_a | real
    data.pools         ordered day-files per pool
    data.person_a      pool -> Parquet event table from Person A's pipeline
    data.timezone      plant-local timezone. Currently NULL and reported as
                       "unconfirmed": the real files span 16:00 to 16:00,
                       which fits UTC+8 and not Europe/Rome. Person A must
                       confirm with AROL - every per-day and per-shift KPI
                       depends on it
    analytics.*        thresholds (sigma, idle window, min_n, torque range)
    agent.planner      rules | llm
    agent.llm.*        host, model, seed, timeout, fallback_to_rules


REPOSITORY LAYOUT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    config.yaml            all paths, thresholds and parameters
    src/common/            schema, envelope, registry, config, datasource,
                           time helpers
    src/ingestion/         api.py (Person A's stub) and adapter.py, which
                           converts his event table to the contract
    src/analytics/         analysis tools (Person B: the KPI half)
    src/agent/             orchestrator, planner, report assembler, trace log
    src/interface/         CLI, plots, HTML/PDF export
    src/testing/           synthetic generator, scaling benchmark
    scripts/               refresh_person_a_cache.py - regenerates the real
                           event table by running Person A's pipeline
    tests/                 test suite, plus person_a_bridge.py which runs his
                           pipeline in a subprocess for the live integration
    docs/                  contract, audit, architecture, benchmark, slides
    reports/               generated reports    (gitignored)
    logs/                  generated traces     (gitignored)


DIVISION OF WORK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Person A   ingestion (WP1), statistical tools: torque distributions,
               drift detection, correlation, torque-based anomalies
    Person B   KPI tools, orchestrator, planner, report assembler, trace log,
               CLI and chat, synthetic generator, integration tests
    Both       the integration contract in docs/contract.md, tool schemas,
               agent prompts, the demo and the presentation

The two halves meet at the event table and the tool registry, both defined in
docs/contract.md. Neither side may change that shape alone.


STATUS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
141 tests passing.

Working now:
    event-table schema and validation, bitfield status decoding, the tool
    registry with generated JSON schemas and enforced parameter types, six
    KPI tools, BOTH planners (rule-based and a local Llama 3.2 3B through
    Ollama, with automatic fallback), the orchestrator loop with cumulative
    retry and graceful degradation, the report assembler, the trace log, the
    CLI, plots and HTML/PDF export, the synthetic generator with ground
    truth, the scaling benchmark, and the adapter for Person A's real event
    table - verified against his actual pipeline on real telemetry.

Not yet built:
    Person A's cleaning stage (objective 2), his deduplication (objective 3),
    his Cloud sync (objective 1 - Parquet persistence exists, the sync step
    does not), and his statistical tools. src/ingestion/api.py is still a
    stub; data.source: person_a is the working path in the meantime.

Open question that matters:
    The plant timezone is unconfirmed. See CONFIGURATION above and
    docs/PROGRESS.md section 5.
